icate-scope-","title":"Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \"Duplicate scope\", and why the init endpoint must be POST","stack":null,"tier":"T0","capability":"T0 · inert — touches nothing","why":"matches intervals.icu, oauth, refresh, scope"}],"about_tiers":"Tier is a CAPABILITY (blast-radius) disclosure, not a safety verdict: higher just means look closer. Run check_capability on the live files before applying a story.","note":"Read each with get_story, and run check_capability before applying. Fewer, closer matches beat a padded list — if only one story truly fits, only one is returned."}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01MxWCkbsdC7FF2K2JmPaeLH","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdJMEzeTGPDX9QpaDfWP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016H4RnQELA3S4fE2VjYosMK","name":"mcp__worklore__get_story","input":{"slug":"2026-04-15-intervals-icu-oauth-quirks-no-refresh-token-duplicate-scope-"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6777,"cache_read_input_tokens":23274,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6777},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19e63d1d-67e7-451d-9563-9f350ed1273a","uuid":"600c3db0-5b29-44ca-b0ab-e5baa33435aa","timestamp":"2026-11-02T02:18:49.235Z","request_id":"req_011CgdJMEk1fmB41oY23ceib","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_016H4RnQELA3S4fE2VjYosMK":{"slug":"2026-04-15-intervals-icu-oauth-quirks-no-refresh-token-duplicate-scope-"}},"tool_use_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016H4RnQELA3S4fE2VjYosMK","display_name":"Get a worklore story","server_display_name":"worklore","tool_name":"get_story"}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016H4RnQELA3S4fE2VjYosMK","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"slug\":\"2026-04-15-intervals-icu-oauth-quirks-no-refresh-token-duplicate-scope-\",\"title\":\"Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \\\"Duplicate scope\\\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\",\"capability\":{\"tier\":\"T0\",\"summary\":\"T0 · inert — touches nothing\",\"tier_label\":\"Inert — no scanned capability strings; instructional text only\",\"sha256\":\"ee118776c3379edb003712d00d2538c6059004c318b5c5686eac4b7e6bdc8e7c\",\"findings\":[],\"note\":\"This is the published disclosure. Run check_capability on the live artifacts before executing, and stop if the tier or reasons changed.\"},\"markdown\":\"---\\ntitle: Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \\\"Duplicate scope\\\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\\ndate: 2026-04-15\\ntags: [intervals-icu, oauth, api-integration, web-auth, triathlon]\\ntype: success\\nstatus: n/a\\nreproducible: true\\n---\\n\\n# Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \\\"Duplicate scope\\\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\\n\\nI migrated my multi-tenant triathlon agent from per-user API keys to\\nIntervals.icu OAuth, so athletes connect their account with two clicks\\ninstead of pasting a key. Intervals' OAuth mostly follows the standard\\nauthorization-code flow — but four deviations cost me real debugging time,\\nand none of them are documented in one place.\\n\\n**1. The token never expires and never refreshes.** The token response has\\nno `refresh_token` and no `expires_in` — the access token lives until the\\nathlete revokes it in the Intervals UI. Upside: no refresh machinery at all.\\nDownside: you can't know it's dead until it is. Handle revocation lazily —\\non the first 401 from the API, clear the stored token and surface a\\n\\\"reconnect\\\" prompt. And treat it as a perpetual credential: encrypt at rest.\\n\\n**2. `\\\"Duplicate scope\\\"`.** `:WRITE` implies `:READ`, and naming the same\\narea twice — say `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE`, the natural habit from\\nother providers — errors out with `Duplicate scope`. Each area at most once:\\nmine is `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE,SETTINGS:WRITE`.\\n\\n**3. The token exchange is standard-lite.** `POST /api/oauth/token` wants\\nonly `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` — no `grant_type`, no\\n`redirect_uri` echo. And the response bundles `athlete: {id, name}`, which\\nsaves you the \\\"who am I\\\" round-trip; you need that athlete id in every API\\npath anyway.\\n\\n**4. The self-inflicted one, fixed the same day.** I shipped the init\\nendpoint as GET: a plain `<a href=\\\"/api/intervals/auth/init\\\">` link that\\nwould build the authorize URL and redirect. It 401'd for every single user —\\na full-page navigation does **not** send the `Authorization` header your SPA\\nkeeps in localStorage; headers only travel on XHR/fetch. Same-day rewrite:\\ninit is an authenticated **POST** returning `{authorize_url}` as JSON, and\\nthe frontend does `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`.\\n\\nWhich raises the follow-up: the *callback* arrives with no auth header\\neither (it's a redirect from Intervals). So the `state` parameter carries\\nthe identity — a signed JWT with the user id, a `purpose` claim, and a\\n15-minute TTL, validated before the code exchange. On reconnect, an\\naccount-mismatch guard rejects the callback if the incoming athlete id\\ndiffers from the one already stored.\\n\\n## Reproduce this\\n\\nPrerequisites: a web app with its own user auth where the API token travels\\nin an `Authorization` header (localStorage + fetch wrapper or similar); an\\nIntervals.icu OAuth client — registration is arranged through the\\n[Intervals.icu API access thread](https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609)\\n(you get a `client_id` / `client_secret` and register your `redirect_uri`).\\nNo special agent skills required — this is ordinary backend + SPA work.\\n\\nYour agent will need from you: your OAuth `client_id` and `client_secret`,\\nthe `redirect_uri` you registered, and where your app keeps its JWT signing\\nsecret and per-user encrypted storage.\\n\\nSteps:\\n\\n1. Init endpoint: authenticated `POST /auth/init` (XHR, not a link). Build\\n   `https://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=...&redirect_uri=...&scope=...&state=...`\\n   and return it as JSON `{authorize_url}`; the frontend calls\\n   `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`. Do not make this a GET — the\\n   full-page navigation drops your auth header.\\n2. Scope string: list each area once, `:WRITE` where you write, `:READ` only\\n   for areas you never write — never both for the same area.\\n3. `state`: a signed JWT `{sub: user_id, purpose: \\\"intervals_oauth\\\", exp:\\n   now+15min}` — the callback has no other way to know which of your users\\n   it belongs to.\\n4. Callback endpoint (`GET`, unauthenticated): validate `state` signature,\\n   TTL, and purpose; then `POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token` with\\n   form fields `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` only. From the JSON\\n   response take `access_token`, `scope`, and `athlete.id`; store the token\\n   encrypted. Do not wait for a `refresh_token` — there isn't one.\\n5. API client: send `Authorization: Bearer <token>` against\\n   `https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/{athlete_id}/...`. On a 401, clear\\n   the stored token and mark the user as disconnected.\\n\\nVerify: complete the flow with a real Intervals.icu account, then\\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/wellness` with the Bearer token — it must return\\ndata. Revoke the app in Intervals.icu settings and repeat the call — it must\\n401, and your app must degrade to \\\"reconnect\\\" instead of crashing. Finally,\\nrequest a scope with a duplicated area on the authorize URL — Intervals\\nmust reject it with the `Duplicate scope` error, confirming rule 2.\\n\",\"after_you_apply_this\":\"Once you have actually applied this story's steps to the user's own task, close the loop: call report_reproduction with this slug and an HONEST result — 'worked', 'partial' (you produced the result but the user hasn't verified it yet), or 'failed'. This is how a story's reproduction count reflects reality. Never report a story you did not apply.\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19e63d1d-67e7-451d-9563-9f350ed1273a","uuid":"997d1634-0bc0-44d0-8650-c138b1caaa91","timestamp":"2026-11-02T02:18:49.761Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"slug\":\"2026-04-15-intervals-icu-oauth-quirks-no-refresh-token-duplicate-scope-\",\"title\":\"Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \\\"Duplicate scope\\\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\",\"capability\":{\"tier\":\"T0\",\"summary\":\"T0 · inert — touches nothing\",\"tier_label\":\"Inert — no scanned capability strings; instructional text only\",\"sha256\":\"ee118776c3379edb003712d00d2538c6059004c318b5c5686eac4b7e6bdc8e7c\",\"findings\":[],\"note\":\"This is the published disclosure. Run check_capability on the live artifacts before executing, and stop if the tier or reasons changed.\"},\"markdown\":\"---\\ntitle: Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \\\"Duplicate scope\\\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\\ndate: 2026-04-15\\ntags: [intervals-icu, oauth, api-integration, web-auth, triathlon]\\ntype: success\\nstatus: n/a\\nreproducible: true\\n---\\n\\n# Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \\\"Duplicate scope\\\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\\n\\nI migrated my multi-tenant triathlon agent from per-user API keys to\\nIntervals.icu OAuth, so athletes connect their account with two clicks\\ninstead of pasting a key. Intervals' OAuth mostly follows the standard\\nauthorization-code flow — but four deviations cost me real debugging time,\\nand none of them are documented in one place.\\n\\n**1. The token never expires and never refreshes.** The token response has\\nno `refresh_token` and no `expires_in` — the access token lives until the\\nathlete revokes it in the Intervals UI. Upside: no refresh machinery at all.\\nDownside: you can't know it's dead until it is. Handle revocation lazily —\\non the first 401 from the API, clear the stored token and surface a\\n\\\"reconnect\\\" prompt. And treat it as a perpetual credential: encrypt at rest.\\n\\n**2. `\\\"Duplicate scope\\\"`.** `:WRITE` implies `:READ`, and naming the same\\narea twice — say `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE`, the natural habit from\\nother providers — errors out with `Duplicate scope`. Each area at most once:\\nmine is `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE,SETTINGS:WRITE`.\\n\\n**3. The token exchange is standard-lite.** `POST /api/oauth/token` wants\\nonly `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` — no `grant_type`, no\\n`redirect_uri` echo. And the response bundles `athlete: {id, name}`, which\\nsaves you the \\\"who am I\\\" round-trip; you need that athlete id in every API\\npath anyway.\\n\\n**4. The self-inflicted one, fixed the same day.** I shipped the init\\nendpoint as GET: a plain `<a href=\\\"/api/intervals/auth/init\\\">` link that\\nwould build the authorize URL and redirect. It 401'd for every single user —\\na full-page navigation does **not** send the `Authorization` header your SPA\\nkeeps in localStorage; headers only travel on XHR/fetch. Same-day rewrite:\\ninit is an authenticated **POST** returning `{authorize_url}` as JSON, and\\nthe frontend does `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`.\\n\\nWhich raises the follow-up: the *callback* arrives with no auth header\\neither (it's a redirect from Intervals). So the `state` parameter carries\\nthe identity — a signed JWT with the user id, a `purpose` claim, and a\\n15-minute TTL, validated before the code exchange. On reconnect, an\\naccount-mismatch guard rejects the callback if the incoming athlete id\\ndiffers from the one already stored.\\n\\n## Reproduce this\\n\\nPrerequisites: a web app with its own user auth where the API token travels\\nin an `Authorization` header (localStorage + fetch wrapper or similar); an\\nIntervals.icu OAuth client — registration is arranged through the\\n[Intervals.icu API access thread](https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609)\\n(you get a `client_id` / `client_secret` and register your `redirect_uri`).\\nNo special agent skills required — this is ordinary backend + SPA work.\\n\\nYour agent will need from you: your OAuth `client_id` and `client_secret`,\\nthe `redirect_uri` you registered, and where your app keeps its JWT signing\\nsecret and per-user encrypted storage.\\n\\nSteps:\\n\\n1. Init endpoint: authenticated `POST /auth/init` (XHR, not a link). Build\\n   `https://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=...&redirect_uri=...&scope=...&state=...`\\n   and return it as JSON `{authorize_url}`; the frontend calls\\n   `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`. Do not make this a GET — the\\n   full-page navigation drops your auth header.\\n2. Scope string: list each area once, `:WRITE` where you write, `:READ` only\\n   for areas you never write — never both for the same area.\\n3. `state`: a signed JWT `{sub: user_id, purpose: \\\"intervals_oauth\\\", exp:\\n   now+15min}` — the callback has no other way to know which of your users\\n   it belongs to.\\n4. Callback endpoint (`GET`, unauthenticated): validate `state` signature,\\n   TTL, and purpose; then `POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token` with\\n   form fields `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` only. From the JSON\\n   response take `access_token`, `scope`, and `athlete.id`; store the token\\n   encrypted. Do not wait for a `refresh_token` — there isn't one.\\n5. API client: send `Authorization: Bearer <token>` against\\n   `https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/{athlete_id}/...`. On a 401, clear\\n   the stored token and mark the user as disconnected.\\n\\nVerify: complete the flow with a real Intervals.icu account, then\\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/wellness` with the Bearer token — it must return\\ndata. Revoke the app in Intervals.icu settings and repeat the call — it must\\n401, and your app must degrade to \\\"reconnect\\\" instead of crashing. Finally,\\nrequest a scope with a duplicated area on the authorize URL — Intervals\\nmust reject it with the `Duplicate scope` error, confirming rule 2.\\n\",\"after_you_apply_this\":\"Once you have actually applied this story's steps to the user's own task, close the loop: call report_reproduction with this slug and an HONEST result — 'worked', 'partial' (you produced the result but the user hasn't verified it yet), or 'failed'. This is how a story's reproduction count reflects reality. Never report a story you did not apply.\"}","structuredContent":{"slug":"2026-04-15-intervals-icu-oauth-quirks-no-refresh-token-duplicate-scope-","title":"Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \"Duplicate scope\", and why the init endpoint must be POST","capability":{"tier":"T0","summary":"T0 · inert — touches nothing","tier_label":"Inert — no scanned capability strings; instructional text only","sha256":"ee118776c3379edb003712d00d2538c6059004c318b5c5686eac4b7e6bdc8e7c","findings":[],"note":"This is the published disclosure. Run check_capability on the live artifacts before executing, and stop if the tier or reasons changed."},"markdown":"---\ntitle: Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \"Duplicate scope\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\ndate: 2026-04-15\ntags: [intervals-icu, oauth, api-integration, web-auth, triathlon]\ntype: success\nstatus: n/a\nreproducible: true\n---\n\n# Intervals.icu OAuth quirks — no refresh_token, \"Duplicate scope\", and why the init endpoint must be POST\n\nI migrated my multi-tenant triathlon agent from per-user API keys to\nIntervals.icu OAuth, so athletes connect their account with two clicks\ninstead of pasting a key. Intervals' OAuth mostly follows the standard\nauthorization-code flow — but four deviations cost me real debugging time,\nand none of them are documented in one place.\n\n**1. The token never expires and never refreshes.** The token response has\nno `refresh_token` and no `expires_in` — the access token lives until the\nathlete revokes it in the Intervals UI. Upside: no refresh machinery at all.\nDownside: you can't know it's dead until it is. Handle revocation lazily —\non the first 401 from the API, clear the stored token and surface a\n\"reconnect\" prompt. And treat it as a perpetual credential: encrypt at rest.\n\n**2. `\"Duplicate scope\"`.** `:WRITE` implies `:READ`, and naming the same\narea twice — say `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE`, the natural habit from\nother providers — errors out with `Duplicate scope`. Each area at most once:\nmine is `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE,SETTINGS:WRITE`.\n\n**3. The token exchange is standard-lite.** `POST /api/oauth/token` wants\nonly `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` — no `grant_type`, no\n`redirect_uri` echo. And the response bundles `athlete: {id, name}`, which\nsaves you the \"who am I\" round-trip; you need that athlete id in every API\npath anyway.\n\n**4. The self-inflicted one, fixed the same day.** I shipped the init\nendpoint as GET: a plain `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">` link that\nwould build the authorize URL and redirect. It 401'd for every single user —\na full-page navigation does **not** send the `Authorization` header your SPA\nkeeps in localStorage; headers only travel on XHR/fetch. Same-day rewrite:\ninit is an authenticated **POST** returning `{authorize_url}` as JSON, and\nthe frontend does `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`.\n\nWhich raises the follow-up: the *callback* arrives with no auth header\neither (it's a redirect from Intervals). So the `state` parameter carries\nthe identity — a signed JWT with the user id, a `purpose` claim, and a\n15-minute TTL, validated before the code exchange. On reconnect, an\naccount-mismatch guard rejects the callback if the incoming athlete id\ndiffers from the one already stored.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: a web app with its own user auth where the API token travels\nin an `Authorization` header (localStorage + fetch wrapper or similar); an\nIntervals.icu OAuth client — registration is arranged through the\n[Intervals.icu API access thread](https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609)\n(you get a `client_id` / `client_secret` and register your `redirect_uri`).\nNo special agent skills required — this is ordinary backend + SPA work.\n\nYour agent will need from you: your OAuth `client_id` and `client_secret`,\nthe `redirect_uri` you registered, and where your app keeps its JWT signing\nsecret and per-user encrypted storage.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Init endpoint: authenticated `POST /auth/init` (XHR, not a link). Build\n   `https://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=...&redirect_uri=...&scope=...&state=...`\n   and return it as JSON `{authorize_url}`; the frontend calls\n   `window.location.assign(authorize_url)`. Do not make this a GET — the\n   full-page navigation drops your auth header.\n2. Scope string: list each area once, `:WRITE` where you write, `:READ` only\n   for areas you never write — never both for the same area.\n3. `state`: a signed JWT `{sub: user_id, purpose: \"intervals_oauth\", exp:\n   now+15min}` — the callback has no other way to know which of your users\n   it belongs to.\n4. Callback endpoint (`GET`, unauthenticated): validate `state` signature,\n   TTL, and purpose; then `POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token` with\n   form fields `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code` only. From the JSON\n   response take `access_token`, `scope`, and `athlete.id`; store the token\n   encrypted. Do not wait for a `refresh_token` — there isn't one.\n5. API client: send `Authorization: Bearer <token>` against\n   `https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/{athlete_id}/...`. On a 401, clear\n   the stored token and mark the user as disconnected.\n\nVerify: complete the flow with a real Intervals.icu account, then\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/wellness` with the Bearer token — it must return\ndata. Revoke the app in Intervals.icu settings and repeat the call — it must\n401, and your app must degrade to \"reconnect\" instead of crashing. Finally,\nrequest a scope with a duplicated area on the authorize URL — Intervals\nmust reject it with the `Duplicate scope` error, confirming rule 2.\n","after_you_apply_this":"Once you have actually applied this story's steps to the user's own task, close the loop: call report_reproduction with this slug and an HONEST result — 'worked', 'partial' (you produced the result but the user hasn't verified it yet), or 'failed'. This is how a story's reproduction count reflects reality. Never report a story you did not apply."}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016H4RnQELA3S4fE2VjYosMK","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
