{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdJReStMcv4KrqqbeCnp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've fixed the Intervals.icu connection; the full flow now works against a local stand-in for Intervals. I haven't tried it against the real intervals.icu. There were three separate causes, written up in `ANSWER.md`:\n\n1. **The Connect button always got \"unauthorized\".** It was a plain link, and clicking a link loads a new page without the sign-in token, which the app only sends on its own background requests. So every athlete got turned away before anything reached Intervals. Now the button asks the server for the Intervals link through the app's normal request path (which carries the token), then sends the browser there. Changes are in `server.py` and `static/app.js`.\n\n2. **Pasting the Intervals link by hand broke on 2026-10-02.** The calendar sync release changed the permission list to name ACTIVITY and CALENDAR twice each (once for read, once for write). Intervals accepts each area only once, and \"write\" already includes \"read\". It rejects repeats with a bare \"invalid_request\" error, which is why nobody saw a consent screen. The list in `config.py` is now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`, which still covers everything the app reads and writes. Rivera's working September connection was granted one entry per area, which fits.\n\n3. **Rivera was told to reconnect after an hour.** Intervals tokens don't expire: they last until the athlete removes the app in Intervals, and they come with no expiry time or renewal token. The server assumed a one-hour lifetime when none was given, then marked the connection expired because it had no way to renew it. Now a connection with no expiry stays valid. If the athlete does remove the app, the next failed request to Intervals marks them \"expired, reconnect\".\n\n**Test:** I ran the server against a temporary mock that rejects repeated permission areas and issues tokens the way Intervals does. Connecting worked, wellness data loaded, and after a simulated revoke the status changed to \"expired, reconnect\". I deleted the mock afterwards.\n\n**Things to deal with separately:**\n- Rivera and anyone else expired by the one-hour timer will need to reconnect once with the fixed button. The old code deleted their stored token.\n- A client secret is hard-coded in `config.py`, and the staging client secret is written out in `README.md`. They should be rotated and moved into environment variables.\n\nI used the matching worklore story (\"Intervals.icu OAuth quirks\"). It asks for a follow-up report back to the service, which I skipped because your rules say not to report anything externally."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1718,"cache_read_input_tokens":37848,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1718},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19e63d1d-67e7-451d-9563-9f350ed1273a","uuid":"ff0977e6-6c4b-40e6-87a7-624e96e39992","timestamp":"2026-11-02T02:19:56.885Z","request_id":"req_011CgdJReCFuetWZ6ZAfpRYS"}
